Behaviours:
Durable usage history:
- Keep scanned usage history with no age limit by default: a missing usageHistoryRetentionDays decodes to "unlimited", so the retention cutoff is epoch zero and nothing is pruned; retain already scanned records even when original transcripts disappear, and keep their directories discoverable for aggregation. An explicitly configured number of days still prunes through upstream's setting.
- Preserve existing source aggregation and deduplication, reuse cached record arrays when no tail is present, and cooperatively yield every 1000 cache entries/records during large scans.

With the default, the provider reader contract receives an epoch-zero retention cutoff; the Cursor account cache reads back to 2023-01-01, before which no Cursor usage exists, whenever the retention start would be earlier; verify all-time Cursor fetch, refresh and restart.

All-time web/desktop range:
- Add a persisted all-time range using a 1970-01-01 query start, support it in both period controls and refresh behavior, and trim chart enumeration to the earliest returned usage day or today for empty results.
- Retain hourly handling for the 24-hour range and existing finite-range shortcuts; let all-time appear without inventing a keybinding and update shortcut type handling and Settings keybinding iteration.
- Document all-time usage, indefinite cached history by default and the setting that limits it, update retained-source/preference tests, preserve current scan-cache tests and add focused all-time window/chart coverage.

Integration details:
- Preserve upstream v11 scan-cache migration, service-tier reconciliation, route-modal layout, and relocated wide/compact period controls while turning off only history-age retention by default.

- Skip retained deleted-transcript cache entries whose mtime precedes the requested window start, while retaining their records indefinitely for all-time queries. Finite-range source file/session counts exclude those entries; align existing out-of-window source assertions.

- Also preserve interrupted/compressed Codex reconciliation, model-filtered scans and summaries, Cursor API-key authentication and consolidated warning messages.
- Upstream removed the Cursor CLI credential and Keychain fallbacks (#32448), so the all-time Cursor history test signs in through CURSOR_API_KEY like the upstream Cursor tests.

Port to 965ddc1d6c:
- Applied without conflicts. UsageService.ts and usageScanCache.ts did not change upstream; 965ddc1d6c still prunes at CACHE_RETENTION_DAYS = 92 (UsageService.ts:73) and has no all-time range.

Port to 691b9709b0:
- Upstream c03f84bcde streams the scan cache to disk and removes superseded cache files after a save, and 729f6757d9 bumps the cache to v12 and merges repeated Claude keys in dedupeWithinFile. Kept both; removed only CACHE_RETENTION_DAYS, pruneScanCache and the retention cutoffs again, next to upstream's STALE_TEMP_DIRECTORY_MS (temp directories, not usage records). The streamed writer and decodeScanCache drop no entry by age.
- An unreadable or oversized usage scan cache is never overwritten: a current file whose content cannot load (empty, invalid JSON, too long to read) is moved aside to usage-scan-cache-v12.unreadable-<time>-<nonce>.json with one warning and saving starts over; a read failure that says nothing about the content (permissions, I/O) of the current file, or a failed move, leaves it in place and stops saving until restart; older-version cache files are kept after a save, as a fallback that may hold usage the saved cache lacks, and one that cannot be loaded is skipped with a warning while saving continues; the writer refuses a document longer than the loader's string limit, keeping the previous file.

Port to e07585e5fb:
- Upstream #33993 added saved scan coverage (coveredSince) and a provisional from-memory read, #35497 replaces archived Codex cache entries, and #35423, #35497 and #29366 bump the cache to v15. Kept all three; removed CACHE_RETENTION_DAYS, pruneScanCache and its coverage clamp, and the retention cutoff in resolveTranscriptDirs, collectDirs and readSummary again. Coverage is never lowered because nothing is pruned.
- The 1,000-item cooperative yield now wraps upstream's per-folder withinDir check and runs before upstream's saved-file reordering; the unreadable-cache set-aside and freeze keep their place in ensureScanCacheLoaded next to upstream's `current` flag.
- UsageService.test.ts: upstream moved these cases from it.live with TestClock.layer() to it.effect (#35868, #35993). The all-time Cursor, cache-upgrade (now v4 to v14, expecting usage-scan-cache-v15.json), saved-usage and saved-window cases follow that form; the upgrade case still expects the older cache files to stay.

Port to ee4ef06469:
- UsageService.test.ts: upstream #36367 bumps the scan cache to v16. The cache-upgrade case keeps this commit's resave and kept-older-files assertions and now expects usage-scan-cache-v16.json.

Port to f7b0b3c4d7: UsageService.test.ts: upstream #30694 (Grok turns from resumed sessions) bumps the scan cache to v17. The cache-upgrade case keeps this commit's resave and kept-older-files assertions and now expects usage-scan-cache-v17.json.

Port to 25875c11b2: Upstream #28111 (428d365932) adds the usageHistoryRetentionDays server setting (a positive number of days or "unlimited", forced to "unlimited" while settings.json cannot be parsed), prunes the scan cache through pruneScanCache inside settingsService.withSettingsSnapshot only when the setting did not change mid-scan, and passes the cutoff to the Cursor account cache.
- Taken from upstream as they are: the setting, its parse-failure override, pruneScanCache, the settings-snapshot guard with its coverage clamp, the computed retentionCutoffMs and its use in resolveTranscriptDirs, collectDirs, reader.scan and the retained-entry filter (retainedSinceMs), and the Cursor account cache freshness check.
- Dropped from this commit: removing CACHE_RETENTION_DAYS (upstream removed it identically), and removing pruneScanCache, its call and coverage clamp, the retentionCutoffMs parameters of resolveTranscriptDirs and collectDirs, the hard-coded retentionCutoffMs: 0 passed to reader.scan and the retainedSinceMs clamp; with the fork default each of these upstream paths evaluates to the old epoch-zero behaviour.
- packages/contracts/src/settings.ts: the decoding default of usageHistoryRetentionDays is "unlimited" instead of 92, so a host whose settings.json lacks the key prunes nothing.
- Kept on top of upstream: the 1,000-item cooperative yield, the unreadable-cache set-aside and freeze, older cache files kept after a save, the writer length limit, the all-time web/desktop range, its query start, chart trimming and shortcut handling.
- UsageService.test.ts: upstream's retention table now expects the default to keep all 15 tokens of deleted transcripts after a restart and refresh, with a new explicit 92-day row that keeps 1 (the later 1-day setting still prunes everything); the Cursor cutoff table expects epoch zero for the default and gains an explicit 92-day row; the retention-race case configures 92 days explicitly so the widening still matters. The all-time Cursor case stays next to upstream's cutoff table.
- serverSettings.test.ts: the saved-usage-settings case checks that a settings.json without the key reads back as "unlimited", both from the service and decoded from the file (it fails with upstream's default of 92).
- docs/user/usage.md: history is kept indefinitely by default, the setting limits it, and the all-time range shows all available history.

Port to b6ac2449f1: the All period follows upstream's translation (055b44919e): its label is msg("All") and its shortcut title resolves through useMessages, and "All time" and the range label use gt() with upstream's locale-aware "{start} to {end}". Upstream's keybinding label table is keyed by usage commands, so its key type drops the All option's undefined command.

Port to b6ac2449f1: upstream 52f0f8afb8 (#38567) makes CursorUsageAccounts.refreshAccount read a large account in pieces, halving the span down to CURSOR_MIN_FETCH_SPAN_MS after a failed read and looping until the cache reaches the retention start. With this commit's epoch-zero default (and upstream's "unlimited") that walk heads back toward 1970 and does not finish.
- packages/provider-cursor: CursorUsageAccounts.scan raises the retention start to CURSOR_HISTORY_START_MS = 2023-01-01T00:00:00Z (accountCache.ts) whenever it would be earlier. Cursor's first release came out in March 2023, and the reader asks GetFilteredUsageEvents for startDate/endDate in epoch milliseconds, so no Cursor usage event predates the floor; the start of 2023 leaves a margin before the launch. A number of days whose cutoff falls after the floor keeps upstream's cutoff; a very large one (36500 days) gets the floor like "unlimited". All-time usage stays all-time: transcript readers still get epoch zero, and the window filter is unchanged.
- Cached Cursor records are pruned only below the floor, where Cursor has none. An existing cache whose sinceMs is 0 counts as complete (0 is before the floor), its next top-up keeps every record and records the floor as its sinceMs.
- UsageService.test.ts: upstream's "loads Cursor history that cannot be read in one request within the deadline" is pinned to usageHistoryRetentionDays 92. Its fake read fails above 12 hours and its loop allows 10 refreshes, enough to halve down from 92 days but not from the 2023 floor (about 12 halvings, then thousands of reads at the narrowed span), so under the fork default it would test the all-time depth instead of the piecewise read. The new "loads all-time Cursor history too large for one read down to its start" keeps the default setting, fails reads wider than 100 days, and requires all three records, an ok source, the earliest read at the floor and fewer than 30 reads; without the floor it reads down to epoch zero. The Cursor cutoff table expects the floor for the default, unlimited and 36500-day rows, the shared-refresh widening case expects its older read to start at the floor, and the all-time Cursor case reads from the floor.

Port to 21b52875ea: UsageService.ts: upstream 2808e878de (#41157, skipped-record warnings count only the selected dates) filters each directory's retained files to the requested window and turns malformedRecords into timestamps. The window filter now runs inside this commit's loop that yields every 1,000 items, on the files pushed per directory, so retained deleted-transcript records are kept and all-time queries still see them.

Port to 4c8a324e8f: upstream a03c43363b (#43206) condensed docs/user/usage.md into bullet lists. Its Retention bullet now says history is kept indefinitely by default, even after transcripts are deleted, and that a number of days limits it ("unlimited" restores the default); the Quick shortcuts bullet points to All in the period control for all available history. Code unchanged.

